<!-- Website teaching layer for kotlin/language-core/variables.md.
     The original chapter is rendered in full; everything here is added around it. -->

<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">A teammate exposes <code>val selectedTags</code> from a screen's state holder. "It's a <code>val</code>, so nobody can change it." A week later, another screen calls <code>selectedTags.add("archived")</code>, the first screen shows a tag the user never picked, and nobody can find the assignment that did it — because there wasn't one.</p>

The bug wasn't in the list. It was in a belief: that `val` freezes a value. This chapter separates the two things every declaration decides — **can this name be pointed somewhere else**, and **can the thing it points to change** — and then follows each kind of binding down to the bytecode, into Android code, and up to the review decisions a staff engineer makes about state.

<!-- @before First Principles :: animation -->
### A `val` is a fixed arrow, not a frozen box

Picture every variable as a **name with an arrow** to an object somewhere in memory. `val` welds the arrow in place. It says nothing about what happens *inside* the object at the other end.

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">What <code>val</code> actually locks</span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Names (bindings)</span><div class="node" data-el="tags" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>val tags</code></strong><small data-text="tagsNote">declared as MutableList&lt;String&gt;</small></div><div class="node" data-el="reassign" data-art="signal" data-art-role="runtime"><strong><code>tags = mutableListOf()</code></strong><small>try to move the arrow</small></div><div class="node" data-el="view" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>val view: List&lt;String&gt;</code></strong><small>a read-only view of the same list</small></div><div class="node" data-el="other" data-art="worker" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Another component</strong><small>still holds the MutableList</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Objects (values)</span><div class="node" data-el="list" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong>MutableList</strong><small data-text="items">["kotlin"]</small></div><div class="node" data-el="immutable" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>listOf("kotlin")</code></strong><small>an implementation with no mutating methods</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Bind","caption":"val tags = mutableListOf(\"kotlin\"). The name tags now points at one MutableList, and that arrow can never move.","add":["tags","list"],"state":{"tags":"active"}},
{"label":"Mutate the object","caption":"tags.add(\"android\") compiles. You are not moving the arrow; you are changing the object it points to.","state":{"list":"active","tags":""},"text":{"items":"[\"kotlin\", \"android\"]"}},
{"label":"Move the arrow","caption":"tags = mutableListOf() is rejected at compile time. This is the only thing val forbids.","add":["reassign"],"state":{"reassign":"error","list":""}},
{"label":"Hide the mutators","caption":"Exposing the list as List<String> removes add and remove from the type callers see. That is a read-only view, not an immutable list.","remove":["reassign"],"add":["view"],"state":{"view":"active"}},
{"label":"The view still changes","caption":"Anyone who still holds the MutableList can change it, and the read-only view shows the change. A read-only type limits who can write; it does not stop writing.","add":["other"],"state":{"other":"warn","list":"active","view":""},"text":{"items":"[\"kotlin\", \"android\", \"archived\"]"}},
{"label":"Full immutability","caption":"Only when the binding is val AND the object's implementation cannot change (listOf, persistent collections, immutable data classes) is the value truly fixed.","remove":["other","list"],"add":["immutable"],"state":{"immutable":"done","view":"done","tags":"done"},"text":{"tagsNote":"val + an immutable implementation"}}
]</script><figcaption>The opening bug, replayed: the arrow never moved, the object did.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after First Principles :: visual -->
### When does each binding get its value?

The kinds of binding differ mostly in **when** the value comes into existence. Read the timeline left to right: from compiling your code to the moment a line runs.

<figure class="viz"><div class="axis"><span>compile time</span><span>class first used</span><span>object created</span><span>later / first read</span></div><div class="viz-lanes"><div class="lane"><span class="lane-label"><code>const val</code></span><div class="track"><span class="mark" style="--at:0%;--len:22%">copied into callers</span></div></div><div class="lane"><span class="lane-label">top-level <code>val</code></span><div class="track"><span class="mark" style="--at:27%;--len:22%">file class loads</span></div></div><div class="lane"><span class="lane-label">property <code>val</code> / <code>var</code></span><div class="track"><span class="mark" style="--at:52%;--len:22%">constructor runs</span></div></div><div class="lane"><span class="lane-label"><code>lateinit var</code></span><div class="track"><span class="mark" data-tone="ghost" style="--at:52%;--len:22%">empty</span><span class="mark" style="--at:77%;--len:22%">assigned later</span></div></div><div class="lane"><span class="lane-label"><code>by lazy</code></span><div class="track"><span class="mark" data-tone="soft" style="--at:52%;--len:22%">block stored</span><span class="mark" style="--at:77%;--len:22%">first read runs it</span></div></div><div class="lane"><span class="lane-label">local <code>val</code></span><div class="track"><span class="mark" style="--at:77%;--len:22%">line executes</span></div></div></div><figcaption>A dashed mark means "exists, but reading it now fails". <code>lateinit</code> throws if read in that window; <code>by lazy</code> fills the gap itself on the first read.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Historical Context :: bridge -->
The history explains the default. The next section shows exactly what each of the four bindings promises — and what it does not.

<!-- @after Mental Model :: check -->
**Pick the binding.** Decide before opening each answer.

<details><summary>A Fragment field that Hilt fills in before <code>onViewCreated</code>.</summary>

`@Inject lateinit var`. The framework owns initialization, the type stays non-null, and reading it early fails loudly with `UninitializedPropertyAccessException` instead of silently passing `null` around.
</details>

<details><summary>An expensive parser used by one rarely opened screen.</summary>

`val parser by lazy { … }`. Nothing is built until the first read; with the default mode, concurrent first reads still build it only once.
</details>

<details><summary>An analytics event name used across 40 call sites.</summary>

`const val`. It is a compile-time string, so typos fail to compile — and changing it means recompiling every caller, which is the right friction for an analytics contract.
</details>

<details><summary>Screen state that the ViewModel changes and the UI only reads.</summary>

`private val _state = MutableStateFlow(…)` plus `val state: StateFlow<…> = _state.asStateFlow()`. The binding is fixed, the flow's value changes, and only the ViewModel holds the mutable type.
</details>

<!-- @diagram 2570aeb49af05293 -->
### Three decisions, in order

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-stack"><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node is-key"><strong>1 · Will the name be reassigned?</strong><small>No → <code>val</code> (the common case). Yes → <code>var</code>.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Remember</strong><small>This decides the arrow only — the object may still be mutable.</small></div></div><div class="down"></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:4"><div class="node is-key"><strong>Now</strong><small><code>= value</code></small></div><div class="node"><strong>Later, non-null</strong><small><code>lateinit var</code></small></div><div class="node"><strong>On first read</strong><small><code>by lazy { }</code></small></div><div class="node"><strong>At compile time</strong><small><code>const val</code> in an object or top level</small></div></div><div class="down"></div><div class="viz-grid" style="--cols:3"><div class="node is-key"><strong><code>private</code></strong><small>this class or file</small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>internal</code></strong><small>this module</small></div><div class="node"><strong><code>public</code></strong><small>everyone</small></div></div></div><figcaption>2 · When does the value exist? 3 · Who may see it? Most declarations end at <code>val</code> + now + <code>private</code>.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: animation -->
### Why a captured `var` moves to the heap

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">Inside <code>makeCounter()</code></span><div class="stage viz-cols" style="--cols:2"><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Call stack</span><div class="node" data-el="frame" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>makeCounter()</code> frame</strong><small data-text="frameNote">running</small></div><div class="node" data-el="caller" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Caller</strong><small>holds the returned lambda</small></div></div><div class="col"><span class="col-title">Heap</span><div class="node" data-el="ref" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong><code>Ref.IntRef</code></strong><small data-text="value">element = 0</small></div><div class="node" data-el="lambda" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Lambda object</strong><small>field → the same IntRef</small></div></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[
{"label":"Declare","caption":"var count = 0 looks like a stack variable. But it is captured by a lambda that will outlive this call, and the lambda needs to change it.","add":["frame"],"state":{"frame":"active"}},
{"label":"Box it","caption":"So the compiler stores count in a heap object, Ref.IntRef, and every use of count becomes count.element.","add":["ref"],"state":{"ref":"active","frame":""}},
{"label":"Capture","caption":"The returned lambda keeps a reference to that same IntRef — not a copy of the number.","add":["lambda"],"state":{"lambda":"active","ref":""}},
{"label":"Return","caption":"makeCounter() returns and its stack frame disappears. The IntRef survives because the lambda still points at it.","remove":["frame"],"add":["caller"],"state":{"caller":"active","lambda":""}},
{"label":"Call it","caption":"Each call reads and increments element on the heap. That shared, long-lived box is why captured vars cost an allocation and can keep data alive.","state":{"ref":"active","caller":""},"text":{"value":"element = 1, then 2, then 3…"}}
]</script><figcaption>A captured <code>val</code> of an object type needs no box: the lambda can copy the reference, because it can never change.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @before Production Example :: visual -->
### The backing-property shape, as a picture

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow"><div class="node"><strong>UI</strong><small>collects <code>state</code>; can only read</small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node is-key"><strong><code>val state: StateFlow</code></strong><small>public, read-only type</small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node"><strong><code>private val _state: MutableStateFlow</code></strong><small>the only writable handle</small></div><div class="arrow"></div><div class="node"><strong><code>increment()</code>, <code>refresh()</code></strong><small>the only paths that write</small></div></div><figcaption>Reads flow out through a read-only type; writes go in through named intent functions. Both properties are <code>val</code>: the arrows are fixed, while the flow's value changes.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Android Example :: bridge -->
Each of these patterns trades something away. The next sections name those costs, then the ways they fail in production.
